<p>Denmark is entering coalition talks after a parliamentary election left neither the left-leaning red bloc nor the right-leaning blue bloc with the 90 seats needed for a majority in the 179-seat Folketing. After meeting King Frederik X, outgoing prime minister Mette Frederiksen submitted her government’s resignation and was given the first chance to test whether she can form a new majority, setting up what could be lengthy negotiations over Denmark’s next coalition.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/SZrBIK5tQHvhWG3J_OxtrQo-wNetqfZ5ysY0XmuVdItOn2iguFpBdFdR9U2hRU6toxz6U7eM1IudnjtEE7gh9g_xHSAjAEDViVK63bNfXQyoBT9hVIoVnKfQN_8ZcJTOR0VAwMLH88gc958ycSH_8iTu6N6Vu39oCv9e42zAp100-bTcWtgELiCkUOTT0abCguj2jDcPhJV9TsIJd1JutAM3RrAkzF5DLX1VvJ6AXhHr1LCLnppKGZGslB8I3XDRUV0fB-YnX9csEok14Q' alt='Prime Minister Mette Frederiksen takes part in a party leaders debate the day after the parliamentary election at the Confederation of Danish Industry&#x27;s building in Copenhagen, Wednesday, March 25, 2026.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Fragmented parliament: The election returned 12 parties to the Folketing, complicating coalition math from the outset.</li><li>Left-first test: The palace asked Frederiksen to see whether she could assemble a majority with her Social Democrats and leftwing parties.</li><li>Centrist coalition defeated: The Globe and Mail described the outgoing administration as a centrist coalition that lost the election, forcing a search for a different governing formula.</li><li>Incumbency hit: The New York Times said Frederiksen was damaged in part by the burdens of governing during the campaign.</li></ul><blockquote>The world is not waiting for us out there and it has only become more unsettled since the election was called. - Mette Frederiksen</blockquote><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>Mette Frederiksen: She said handing in the cabinet’s resignation was necessary because the outgoing three-party coalition no longer had enough mandates to continue. (<a href='https://www.theguardian.com/world/live/2026/mar/25/denmark-election-results-mette-frederiksen-europe-latest-news-updates'>The Guardian</a>)</li><li>Danish palace: Its statement emphasized the formal post-election process, with royal consultations used to identify who should test parliament for support first. (<a href='https://www.theguardian.com/world/2026/mar/25/denmark-braces-lengthy-challenging-coalition-talks'>The Guardian</a>)</li><li>The New York Times analysis: Its account said voter attention centered on local agricultural issues rather than on Greenland or wider foreign-policy questions. (<a href='https://www.nytimes.com/2026/03/25/world/europe/denmark-elections-pigs-frederiksen.html'>The New York Times</a>)</li><li>The Globe and Mail analysis: Its report suggested that the defeat of Frederiksen’s centrist coalition did not preclude her from remaining in office if coalition arithmetic ultimately favors her. (<a href='https://www.theglobeandmail.com/world/article-denmark-election-mette-frederiksen-coalition/'>The Globe and Mail</a>)</li></ul><h3>Sources:</h3><ul><li><a href='https://www.wsws.org/en/articles/2026/03/25/ulzk-m25.html'>Governing parties suffer losses in Danish election</a> - wsws.org</li><li><a href='https://www.theguardian.com/world/live/2026/mar/25/denmark-election-results-mette-frederiksen-europe-latest-news-updates'>Frederiksen to lead talks on forming new Danish government – as it happened</a> - theguardian.com</li><li><a href='https://www.theglobeandmail.com/world/article-denmark-election-mette-frederiksen-coalition/'>Danish PM Mette Frederiksen to explore left-wing coalition despite election loss</a> - theglobeandmail.com</li><li><a href='https://www.nytimes.com/2026/03/25/world/europe/denmark-elections-pigs-frederiksen.html'>In Denmark, It’s All About the Pigs</a> - nytimes.com</li></ul>